---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'tldr', label: 'TL;DR' },
  { id: 'problem', label: 'Problem' },
  { id: 'system', label: 'System' },
  { id: 'features', label: 'Features' },
  { id: 'validation', label: 'Validation' },
  { id: 'engineering', label: 'Engineering' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'Limitations' },
  { id: 'role', label: 'My role' },
];

const stages = [
  { n: '01', name: 'Ingest', detail: 'Sentinel-2 multi-spectral imagery · 13 bands' },
  { n: '02', name: 'Features', detail: '50–100+ engineered features per point · 10 m grid' },
  { n: '03', name: 'Train', detail: 'Vertex AI GPU training · spatially-blocked validation' },
  { n: '04', name: 'Predict', detail: 'Per-field nutrient predictions · 172+ fields' },
  { n: '05', name: 'Serve', detail: '6 services on GKE · Helm · Cloud Build CI/CD' },
];

const stats = [
  { v: '40 min', l: 'end-to-end run (was multi-day)' },
  { v: '172+', l: 'fields in production' },
  { v: '12+ / 5', l: 'farms / states' },
  { v: '10 m', l: 'feature resolution' },
];
---

<Base
  title="SoilMetrix pipeline — case study"
  description="Production geo-soil ML: an end-to-end pipeline turning Sentinel-2 imagery into field-specific soil-nutrient predictions, from feature engineering to GKE serving."
>
  <header class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 pb-8 pt-16 sm:px-8 sm:pt-20">
    <p class="eyebrow reveal">Flagship case study · production ML</p>
    <h1
      class="reveal mt-4 max-w-3xl text-4xl font-semibold leading-[1.08] tracking-tight sm:text-5xl"
    >
      SoilMetrix: satellite pixels to soil decisions, in 40 minutes
    </h1>
    <p class="reveal mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
      The production counterpart to <a href="/geosoil" class="text-moss-600 underline decoration-moss-300 underline-offset-2 hover:text-moss-700">GeoSoil</a>:
      an end-to-end ML pipeline that turns Sentinel-2 imagery into field-specific
      soil-nutrient predictions for working farms — built and operated as a
      part-time engineer across three years.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-12 px-5 sm:px-8 lg:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]">
    <aside class="hidden lg:block">
      <nav class="sticky top-20 space-y-1.5 border-l border-line pl-4">
        {
          toc.map((t) => (
            <a
              href={`#${t.id}`}
              class="block py-0.5 text-sm text-ink-3 transition-colors hover:text-moss-600"
            >
              {t.label}
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </nav>
    </aside>

    <article class="min-w-0">
      <!-- TL;DR -->
      <section id="tldr" class="scroll-mt-20">
        <div class="reveal rounded-2xl border border-moss-200 bg-moss-50/50 p-6 sm:p-8">
          <p class="eyebrow">TL;DR</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-[1.05rem] leading-relaxed text-ink">
            I designed and built the end-to-end pipeline behind SoilMetrix's soil-nutrient
            predictions: Sentinel-2 ingestion → 50–100+ features per point at 10&nbsp;m →
            Vertex AI training → serving from six services on GKE. It replaced a
            <span class="font-medium">multi-day manual workflow</span> with a
            <span class="tnum font-medium text-moss-700">40-minute</span> automated run and
            delivers production predictions across
            <span class="tnum font-medium text-moss-700">172+ fields</span> on 12+ farms in
            5 states. Validation is spatially blocked — the detail that keeps the accuracy
            numbers honest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="reveal mt-5 grid grid-cols-2 gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-line bg-line sm:grid-cols-4">
          {
            stats.map((s) => (
              <div class="bg-card px-4 py-5">
                <p class="tnum text-2xl font-semibold text-moss-700">{s.v}</p>
                <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-[0.72rem] leading-snug text-ink-3">{s.l}</p>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- PROBLEM -->
      <section id="problem" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-16">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal">01 · Problem</p>
        <h2 class="reveal mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
          Soil sampling doesn't scale; satellites do
        </h2>
        <div class="prose-col mt-5 space-y-4 text-[1.02rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          <p class="reveal">
            Farmers manage inputs field-by-field, but lab soil sampling is sparse and
            slow — a handful of points must speak for hundreds of acres. SoilMetrix's
            bet is that multi-spectral satellite signal, engineered carefully, can
            interpolate between those samples and turn them into wall-to-wall nutrient
            maps a farmer can act on.
          </p>
          <p class="reveal">
            When I joined, producing predictions for a new farm was a multi-day manual
            workflow: data pulls, feature computation, model runs and map exports, each
            step hand-operated. The job was to make it a pipeline — reliable enough for
            production, fast enough to iterate on models daily.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SYSTEM -->
      <section id="system" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-16">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal">02 · System</p>
        <h2 class="reveal mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
          One automated path from imagery to field map
        </h2>
        <div class="reveal mt-6 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-line">
          {
            stages.map((s, i) => (
              <div
                class:list={[
                  'flex items-baseline gap-4 px-5 py-4',
                  i % 2 ? 'bg-card' : 'bg-paper/40',
                  i > 0 ? 'border-t border-line' : '',
                ]}
              >
                <span class="tnum text-xs text-moss-400">{s.n}</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="font-medium text-ink">{s.name}</p>
                  <p class="mt-0.5 font-mono text-[0.78rem] text-ink-3">{s.detail}</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
        <p class="reveal prose-col mt-5 text-[1.02rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          The platform runs as six services on Kubernetes (GKE) spanning eleven GCP
          services — Vertex AI for GPU training, Cloud SQL for state, Cloud Build for
          CI/CD — with Helm charts handling cross-environment deployment. One command
          takes a new farm from raw imagery to served predictions in about 40 minutes.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- FEATURES -->
      <section id="features" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-16">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal">03 · Feature engineering</p>
        <h2 class="reveal mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
          Where the domain knowledge lives
        </h2>
        <div class="prose-col mt-5 space-y-4 text-[1.02rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          <p class="reveal">
            Raw bands are weak predictors on their own; the signal is in the derived
            structure. I authored the core Python package that computes
            <span class="font-medium text-ink">50–100+ features per point at 10&nbsp;m
            resolution</span>: spectral indices from the 13 Sentinel-2 bands, GIS
            derivatives computed with GRASS and PostGIS, and the sampling-grid
            generation that decides where predictions are made.
          </p>
          <p class="reveal">
            The same package owns the soil-sampling algorithms and multi-state
            derivative processing — so agronomy assumptions live in one tested library,
            not scattered across notebooks.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- VALIDATION -->
      <section id="validation" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-16">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal">04 · Validation</p>
        <h2 class="reveal mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
          Spatial blocking, or the metrics lie
        </h2>
        <div class="prose-col mt-5 space-y-4 text-[1.02rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          <p class="reveal">
            Soil is spatially autocorrelated: two points 200&nbsp;m apart are nearly
            duplicates. Randomly split them across train and test and the model is
            graded on its neighbors — metrics inflate, and the field map looks better
            in the backtest than it performs on a new farm. All model evaluation here
            uses <span class="font-medium text-ink">spatially blocked
            cross-validation</span>, so test data is always geographically disjoint
            from training data.
          </p>
          <p class="reveal">
            The research side of this argument — including what happens when popular
            soil benchmarks are themselves model outputs — is made quantitatively in
            the <a href="/geosoil" class="text-moss-600 underline decoration-moss-300 underline-offset-2 hover:text-moss-700">GeoSoil case study</a>,
            where the same spatial-block protocol is applied against three independent
            ground truths.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ENGINEERING -->
      <section id="engineering" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-16">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal">05 · Engineering</p>
        <h2 class="reveal mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
          Boring, repeatable deployment
        </h2>
        <div class="prose-col mt-5 space-y-4 text-[1.02rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          <p class="reveal">
            Retraining and deployment are automated: Cloud Build runs the CI/CD path,
            Helm charts template the six services across environments, and models move
            from Vertex AI training to serving without manual promotion steps. The
            40-minute figure is the whole loop — not just inference — which is what
            makes daily model iteration practical for a part-time team.
          </p>
          <p class="reveal">
            Details here are method-level by design: client names, datasets, and
            infrastructure identifiers are omitted. The methodology, architecture
            reasoning, and my own experiments are what's mine to show — the
            <a href="https://github.com/luker-robinson2/soil-state-transformer" class="text-moss-600 underline decoration-moss-300 underline-offset-2 hover:text-moss-700">public research repo</a>
            demonstrates the same skills on public data.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- LIMITATIONS -->
      <section id="limits" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-16">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal">06 · Limitations</p>
        <h2 class="reveal mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
          What this pipeline doesn't do
        </h2>
        <div class="prose-col mt-5 space-y-4 text-[1.02rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          <p class="reveal">
            Per-pixel uncertainty is not yet a first-class product output — predictions
            ship as point estimates, and quantifying spatial confidence is the clearest
            next step (the conformal machinery explored in GeoSoil is the candidate).
            And like all optical remote sensing, the approach reads surface signal:
            subsurface properties remain bounded by what 13 bands at 10&nbsp;m can see.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ROLE -->
      <section id="role" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-16">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal">07 · My role</p>
        <h2 class="reveal mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
          Solo on the pipeline, part of a product
        </h2>
        <div class="prose-col mt-5 space-y-4 text-[1.02rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          <p class="reveal">
            Data Scientist &amp; ML Engineer, June 2023 – present, part-time throughout
            my BS and into my MS at CU Boulder. I designed and built the end-to-end
            pipeline, authored the core feature-engineering package, and own the
            training and deployment automation. Product, agronomy expertise, and the
            customer relationships are the wider team's.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
